  lastUpdated={new Date("2026-08-05")}
>
  <section>
    <p>Where a card review carries a scorecard, it uses a tier badge, five dimension scores and a one-line Apply / Skip pair. The rubric below is fixed wherever it is applied; pages without a completed rubric do not display a scorecard. It is editorial judgement, not a black-box index.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The five dimensions</h2>

    <p><strong>Welcome value.</strong> The AED-equivalent of the welcome bonus, net of joining fee, on the assumption the reader triggers it inside the qualifying window. A 100,000 Skywards Miles bonus values at AED 2,000 against our 2 fils-per-mile baseline; subtract the AED 3,148.95 joining fee and the headline is net AED -1,148.95 before considering any economy-to-business-class upgrade. High scores reward bonuses that clear the joining fee at realistic spend; low scores flag bonuses that need a stretch trigger.</p>

    <p><strong>Earn rate.</strong> The ongoing miles, points, or cashback against the issuer's published spend denominator. We preserve that denominator, including USD, rather than silently relabelling it as AED. Comparative scoring normalises to AED only when a documented conversion exists; otherwise unlike units are not ranked against one another. High scores reward strong, usable returns for the audience; low scores identify weak ongoing value.</p>

    <p><strong>Perks.</strong> Lounge access, hotel status, golf, cinema, concierge, valet — anything bundled at no extra fee. We weight lounge access and hotel status most, novelty perks least. High scores carry unlimited lounge access with a guest; low scores carry only token discounts.</p>

    <p><strong>Fee value.</strong> Joining fee plus year-two renewal, weighed against the realistic value extracted at the audience the card targets. A high score means the card pays back at modest spend; a low score means the fee only clears at AED 100,000+ a year and bites everyone below.</p>

    <p><strong>Access.</strong> Eligibility friendliness — minimum salary, salary-transfer requirement, documentation burden. A high score means a mid-market expat (AED 10,000–15,000 salary, no salary transfer required) can hold the card. A low score reserves the card for the AED 30,000+ band.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The five tiers</h2>

    <p><strong>Editor's Pick.</strong> The card we would open ourselves at the audience the kicker names — high scores on the dimensions that matter for that audience.</p>

    <p><strong>Strong.</strong> A clear recommendation inside its segment; one or two dimensions hold it back from Editor's Pick.</p>

    <p><strong>Solid.</strong> Does its job for the right reader; no single dimension is weak, none is exceptional.</p>

    <p><strong>Niche.</strong> Pays back only for a narrow use case — a specific airline, a specific spend pattern, a specific lounge network.</p>

    <p><strong>Skip.</strong> The fee, the earn rate, or the access bar cannot be defended against a cheaper alternative on the same shortlist.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>VAT convention</h2>
    <p>Annual and joining fees rendered from normalised card data are inclusive of the UAE's 5% VAT unless explicitly marked otherwise. Editorial comparisons may retain an issuer's ex-VAT amount when the VAT-inclusive equivalent is shown alongside it. Card pages tag inclusive fees "(incl. VAT)" where the source basis requires clarification.</p>
  </section>

